---
// Commercial cleaning page, built from content-plans/commercial-cleaning.md.
//
// THIS PAGE IS A HUB, NOT A LEAF. /commercial-cleaning/ is a top-level
// header item with FIFTEEN child pages under it (navigation.ts:143-172).
// The brief was written as a standalone service page and linked to none of
// them. The "What we clean" TagCloud below is not in the brief — it was
// added because a hub that doesn't link its cluster isn't a hub. See the
// plan's blocker 2.
//
// The audience is B2B: an office manager, practice manager, strata
// committee or venue owner choosing a cleaner for premises they are
// responsible for. Not a householder, and emphatically not an NDIS
// participant — see the §3 note.
//
// BLOCKS LAUNCH:
//   - Screening. Card 3 claims [police-checked] while the last non-branded
//     FAQ tells readers that childcare work needs a Working With Children
//     Check and to "ask any provider to confirm which of their team hold a
//     current check". Those are DIFFERENT checks, the mega-menu actively
//     invites school/childcare enquiries, and the page currently tells the
//     reader to interrogate TLB about something it doesn't answer.
//     Brackets ship visibly per house convention; these must be filled.
//   - QLD is unaddressed. The non-branded FAQ says "In NSW" (correct), but
//     footerContact says "Northern Rivers, NSW & Southern Gold Coast, QLD"
//     and QLD's equivalent is a Blue Card, not a WWCC. Either confirm this
//     page is NSW-only or the answer needs a QLD clause.
//   - The scope of work is missing entirely. See the §8 comment — the page
//     tells the reader to demand a task list four times and never shows one.
//   - Card 2 carries a bracket because nobody has said how TLB invoices
//     commercial clients.
//
// NOT BUILT, deliberately: credibility signals, hero video, "the why" — all
// empty in the brief. Precedent is deep-cleaning.astro, whose credibility
// block was filled on a reasonable assumption and removed again at TLB's
// direction.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/sections/SiteHeader.astro';
import Hero from '../components/sections/Hero.astro';
import TrustBar from '../components/sections/TrustBar.astro';
import TextBlock from '../components/sections/TextBlock.astro';
import PathwayCards from '../components/sections/PathwayCards.astro';
import TagCloud from '../components/sections/TagCloud.astro';
import ComparisonTable from '../components/sections/ComparisonTable.astro';
import TestimonialCarousel from '../components/sections/TestimonialCarousel.astro';
import Faq from '../components/sections/Faq.astro';
import CallToAction from '../components/sections/CallToAction.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/sections/SiteFooter.astro';
import logoImg from '../assets/logo/tlb-logo-horizontal.png';
import footerLogoImg from '../assets/logo/tlb-logo-square.png';
import type { TextBlockBody } from '../components/sections/TextBlock.astro';
import {
  primaryNav,
  headerNav,
  headerSecondaryCta,
  footerServiceLinks,
  footerContact,
  footerCopyright,
  quoteCta,
} from '../data/navigation';
import { comparisonColumns, comparisonRows } from '../data/comparison';

const pageMeta = {
  // Brief said "Commercial Cleaning Service |  TLB Cleaning" — singular, and
  // with a double space. Pluralised to match the focus keyword ("Commercial
  // Cleaning Services") and the double space removed. Same two defects the
  // NDIS brief had, same fix. Flagged in the plan's §0.
  title: 'Commercial Cleaning Services | TLB Cleaning',
  description:
    'Commercial and office cleaning across the Northern Rivers and Tweed. The same local team every week, a scope in writing, and no surprises. Get a quote.',
  canonical: 'https://example.com/commercial-cleaning/',
};

const business = {
  name: 'TLB Cleaning',
  url: 'https://example.com/',
  logo: '',
  telephone: '',
  email: '',
  address: {
    streetAddress: '',
    addressLocality: '',
    addressRegion: '',
    postalCode: '',
    addressCountry: 'AU',
  },
  sameAs: [],
  // ⚠️ NSW only, following the brief's own meta description ("across the
  // Northern Rivers and Tweed"). This contradicts footerContact.address,
  // which claims the Southern Gold Coast too, and it interacts with the
  // Blue Card gap in the last non-branded FAQ. Confirm before launch.
  areaServed: 'Northern Rivers and Tweed, NSW',
  description: pageMeta.description,
};

// §1 Hero — drafted in full. Lead is 23 words, inside the 15-25 target.
//
// ⚠️ KICKER ADDED, not in the brief. The H1 contains no part of the focus
// keyword — neither "commercial", "office" nor "cleaning" appears in it —
// while the brief's own template row asks the headline to "overlap with key
// topic and audience targeting". Someone arriving from a SERP gets no
// confirmation they landed on the right page. Hero's `kicker` renders above
// the <h1>, outside it, so this orients the reader without editing approved
// copy. (Second consumer of the prop after why-tlb.)
//
// ⚠️ CTA LABEL CHANGED from the brief's "Get an instant quote". There is no
// instant quote for commercial work and the page says so twice: branded FAQ
// 5 promises a site walkthrough then a written scope and price, and the
// first non-branded FAQ closes "a site walkthrough settles it in ten
// minutes". The sitewide header CTA is untouched; this is page-level only.
//
// 🟡 `minimal` because no photo is supplied. A real TLB team member in a
// real workplace would do more for a B2B buyer than any sentence here.
// Do NOT use src/assets/hero/* — those are Maple template leftovers, not
// TLB's people.
const hero = {
  kicker: 'Commercial Cleaning',
  headingLines: ['Professional ongoing service that never cuts corners after month one.'],
  lead: 'The reliable middle your office needs. Trust a fully insured local team to keep your workplace spotless, professional, and welcoming week after week.',
  cta: { label: 'Book a site walkthrough', href: quoteCta.href },
};

// §4 Trust bar — the brief's three points, unchanged.
//
// ✅ This is the first page on the site where the third point is genuinely
// on-target. ndis-cleaning.md flagged "Trusted by leading Northern Rivers
// real estate agencies" as meaningless to that audience and quietly
// signalling "commercial cleaning contractor". Here that signal is the
// correct one: an agency is a commercial client, the relationship is an
// ongoing contract, and property managers inspect the work weekly.
//
// ⚠️ OPEN DECISION, not taken here. Point 1 is the fourth wording of this
// claim across five pages; elsewhere it reads "nine full-time cleaners,
// employed not subcontracted". For a commercial reader the
// employed-not-subcontracted phrasing is materially stronger, because
// subcontracting is the exact practice the "Worth knowing" block and the
// comparison table are both about. Left as the brief wrote it rather than
// changing supplied copy unilaterally.
const trustBarItems = [
  '9 full-time local team members',
  'Every client came from a recommendation',
  'Trusted by leading Northern Rivers real estate agencies',
];

// §2 Definition paragraph — the brief supplied NOTHING here (the row held
// only "CTA - Get an instant quote"), but this is the answer-target block
// for the focus keyword, and with the H1 carrying none of that keyword it is
// the first place on the page the phrase can appear in body copy.
//
// ⚠️ DRAFTED, NOT SUPPLIED — needs sign-off. Unlike the NDIS page's
// equivalent gap, nothing here is regulated: this is ordinary marketing
// copy, not a statement about a funded support. Sentence one describes the
// service and names the premises types the mega-menu already commits to;
// sentence two is the standard sibling pattern (what TLB does, where).
const definitionBody: TextBlockBody[] = [
  'Commercial cleaning is the ongoing, contracted cleaning of a workplace — offices, clinics, venues, strata common areas and retail floors — carried out to an agreed scope on a set schedule, usually before opening or after close.',
  'TLB Cleaning provides commercial cleaning services across the Northern Rivers and the Tweed.',
];

// §3 Pathway cards — REWRITTEN. This was the brief's biggest defect.
//
// The three cards were lifted from ndis-cleaning.md and only partly adapted.
// Card 2 was not adapted at all: "We work directly with self-managed and
// plan-managed participants, sending clear, compliant invoices straight to
// your plan manager". Those are NDIS terms. They mean nothing to an office
// manager, and to anyone who recognises them they advertise that this page
// was assembled from another one. It could not ship in any form.
//
// Cards 1 and 3 were contaminated more mildly but still wrong in register —
// "regular support" is disability-sector language where the commercial word
// is service or contract; "who is at the door" describes a house, not a
// workplace with a back entrance and an alarm code; "warmth" is the right
// note for a home and the wrong one for a tenancy.
//
// All three are re-pointed at the three questions a commercial buyer
// actually arrives with, using answers this brief already contains
// elsewhere. Card 3 is near-verbatim from branded FAQ 2, which answered the
// question better than the original card did.
//
// The source titles each ended in a literal "→"; stripped, because
// PathwayCards renders its own CTA affordance and the arrow would duplicate
// it. (Same call as every sibling page.)
const pathwayCards = {
  // Not in the brief — placeholder structural copy.
  heading: 'Whatever you need from a commercial cleaner,',
  headingAccent: 'start here.',
  cards: [
    {
      number: 1,
      image: { ratio: '4/3', label: 'TLB cleaner working in an office after hours' },
      title: 'Set up an ongoing contract',
      description:
        'We agree a weekly, fortnightly or daily schedule and put the scope in writing before we start. The same team works your site each visit, so nobody has to be shown around twice.',
      cta: { label: 'Office cleaning', href: '/office-cleaning/' },
    },
    {
      number: 2,
      image: { ratio: '4/3', label: 'Written cleaning scope and quote on a desk' },
      title: 'I need to know what it costs',
      description:
        // ⚠️ VISIBLE BRACKET, per house convention. The pricing half is drawn
        // from the brief's own "Worth knowing" block and non-branded FAQ 4,
        // both of which TLB wrote. The invoicing half is a genuine unknown —
        // nobody has said how TLB bills commercial clients (monthly in
        // arrears? PO numbers? per visit?). That is input, not a rewrite.
        'We quote against a written scope rather than an hourly rate, so you can see exactly which tasks are included, how often, and whether consumables like soap and paper are covered. [Invoicing terms — confirm how TLB bills commercial clients.]',
      // ⚠️ STOPGAP DESTINATION. What this card wants is a scope-and-pricing
      // block to anchor to, and that content does not exist yet (see §8).
      // /contact/ stops it stranding in the meantime.
      cta: { label: 'Talk to us', href: '/contact/' },
    },
    {
      number: 3,
      image: { ratio: '4/3', label: 'TLB team member in uniform at a commercial site' },
      title: 'I want to know who is in our building',
      description:
        // ⚠️ Two visible brackets, both launch-blocking. See the file header:
        // this page tells readers to ask providers which checks their team
        // hold, so leaving TLB's own answer unresolved is worse here than on
        // a page that never raised the question.
        'The same small team you have met, in TLB uniform — never a roster of strangers sent by a call centre. We are a real local business, [insured] and [police-checked], and there is one local number to call if anything is not right.',
      cta: { label: 'Why TLB', href: '/why-tlb/' },
    },
  ],
};

// §5 "Worth knowing" — ships VERBATIM. This is the strongest copy in any of
// the five briefs and should not be edited down for scannability.
//
// It works because it explains something the reader doesn't know, it is
// honest about TLB's own pricing ("that costs more because the Award says
// so, not because we've added a margin"), and it converts by making the
// reader distrust the cheapest quote rather than by making claims about TLB.
//
// COMPONENT CHOICE: TextBlock (dark), not Callout. Callout is built and
// available, and "read this before you go further" is the right intent — but
// this is 164 words across two paragraphs, where both existing Callout
// consumers are a single short paragraph, and SECTIONS.md is explicit that
// Callout's heading is "visually an aside, not a new chapter". The brief
// marks this row as an H2 with a real section heading. A 164-word aside
// stops reading as an aside.
//
// ⚠️ "Worth knowing" is the boilerplate section LABEL; the heading below is
// the real one, per the row's own copy.
//
// ⚠️ Minor open decision: the Award is named in full ("Cleaning Services
// Award 2020") in the non-branded FAQs but only as "the Cleaning Services
// Award" here, on first mention. Left as written rather than editing
// supplied copy.
const worthKnowingBody: TextBlockBody[] = [
  "Contract cleaners in Australia are covered by the Cleaning Services Award, which sets a legal minimum for what a cleaner has to be paid — before you add super, insurance, equipment and travel. So when one quote lands well under the others, it's rarely a sharper deal. It usually means a shorter visit, a thinner list of tasks, or someone being paid less than they should be. The bill you don't see is the one you pay later, in dirty glass, missed bins and a cleaner who quietly stops showing up.",
  "The number that actually matters isn't the hourly rate — it's what's in the scope. Ask any cleaner exactly which tasks are included, how often, and whether consumables like soap and paper are covered. We put ours in writing before we start, and we'll tell you straight when a clean needs to happen after hours: that costs more because the Award says so, not because we've added a margin. No surprises, either way.",
];

// §7 Premises types — NOT IN THE BRIEF. Added per the plan's blocker 2.
//
// /commercial-cleaning/ is a top-level nav item with fifteen children
// (navigation.ts:143-172) and the brief linked to none of them. A visitor
// who runs a café, a clinic or a strata block had no route to the page
// written for them, and fifteen child pages got no internal link from their
// own parent. That is simultaneously the page's biggest conversion gap and
// its biggest SEO gap.
//
// TagCloud over ServiceBlocks: fifteen items is exactly the "long, flat list
// of categories as pills" TagCloud exists for, and the per-item descriptions
// ServiceBlocks would want don't exist. Fifteen stacked full-width rows
// would also be a very long section.
//
// ⚠️ Every href below is a flat kebab-case GUESS from its menu label, per
// navigation.ts:86-89 — unconfirmed sitewide, and some are odd as standalone
// URLs (/factories/, /breweries/, /construction-site/, the last singular
// where every sibling is plural). Confirm before launch; this section wires
// fifteen more links to them.
//
// Office cleaning is highlighted as the default premises type — it is the
// one the meta description names.
const premisesTags = [
  { label: 'Office cleaning', href: '/office-cleaning/' },
  { label: 'Strata and common area cleaning', href: '/strata-and-common-area-cleaning/' },
  { label: 'Medical, clinic and salon cleaning', href: '/medical-clinic-and-salon-cleaning/' },
  { label: 'Aged care, retirement and seniors', href: '/aged-care-retirement-and-seniors/' },
  { label: 'Hospitality, venues and holiday parks', href: '/hospitality-venues-and-holiday-parks/' },
  { label: 'Commercial kitchen cleaning', href: '/commercial-kitchen-cleaning/' },
  { label: 'Schools and childcare centres', href: '/schools-and-childcare-centres/' },
  { label: 'Gyms and fitness studios', href: '/gyms-and-fitness-studios/' },
  { label: 'Retail and shopfronts', href: '/retail-and-shopfronts/' },
  { label: 'Warehouses and industrial sites', href: '/warehouses-and-industrial-sites/' },
  { label: 'Construction site', href: '/construction-site/' },
  { label: 'Factories', href: '/factories/' },
  { label: 'Breweries', href: '/breweries/' },
  { label: 'Commercial carpet cleaning', href: '/commercial-carpet-cleaning/' },
  { label: 'Commercial pressure cleaning', href: '/commercial-pressure-cleaning/' },
];

// §14 Where we clean — NOT IN THE BRIEF, which had no service-area block at
// all despite every sibling page having one. A commercial page with no
// service area can't rank for "commercial cleaners [suburb]", which is how
// this is searched.
//
// ⚠️ The site already has FOUR different suburb lists. Rather than add a
// fifth, this reuses the header mega-menu's own "Northern Rivers NSW" group
// (navigation.ts:182-195) verbatim, minus its hub link. NSW only, following
// this page's meta description — which is also why the Blue Card gap in the
// last non-branded FAQ matters. If commercial work does cross the border,
// this list and that FAQ both need the Southern Gold Coast added.
const locationTags = [
  { label: 'Byron Bay', href: '/locations/byron-bay/' },
  { label: 'Brunswick Heads', href: '/locations/brunswick-heads/' },
  { label: 'Ballina', href: '/locations/ballina/' },
  { label: 'Lennox Head', href: '/locations/lennox-head/' },
  { label: 'Lismore', href: '/locations/lismore/' },
  { label: 'Alstonville', href: '/locations/alstonville/' },
  { label: 'Kingscliff', href: '/locations/kingscliff/' },
  { label: 'Pottsville', href: '/locations/pottsville/' },
  { label: 'Murwillumbah', href: '/locations/murwillumbah/' },
  { label: 'Evans Head', href: '/locations/evans-head/' },
  { label: 'Casino', href: '/locations/casino/' },
  { label: 'Tweed Heads', href: '/locations/tweed-heads/' },
];

// §9 Social proof — bracketed [TBC] briefing slots, NOT real testimonials.
//
// ⚠️ The brief supplied NOTHING here — not even the "ask Teagan for
// testimonials" line house-cleaning and deep-cleaning both carried. The
// three angles below are a proposal; confirm before briefing Teagan.
//
// Commercial testimonials do a different job from household ones: a
// household quote proves the clean was good, a commercial quote has to prove
// the RELATIONSHIP held up. A named business and a role ("practice manager",
// "centre director") carries far more weight in B2B than a first name — and
// unlike the NDIS page there is no sensitivity reason to withhold it, though
// it still needs the client's consent.
//
// Never invent a quote or a business name.
const testimonials = [
  {
    quote:
      '[TBC — ask Teagan for a quote from a client who switched from a national or franchise cleaner. This is the one that pays off branded FAQ 3. A named business and role, with consent.]',
    name: '[Business name — TBC]',
    role: '[Role, e.g. practice manager — TBC]',
    rating: 5,
    avatar: { ratio: '1/1', label: 'TLB commercial client' },
  },
  {
    quote:
      "[TBC — ask Teagan for a quote from a client two or three years in, whose standard did not drop. This pays off the H1's \"never cuts corners after month one\" and branded FAQ 4.]",
    name: '[Business name — TBC]',
    role: '[Role — TBC]',
    rating: 5,
    avatar: { ratio: '1/1', label: 'Long-standing TLB commercial client' },
  },
  {
    quote:
      '[TBC — ask Teagan for a quote from a real estate agency or property manager. The trust bar already claims this relationship and nothing on the site evidences it.]',
    name: '[Agency name — TBC]',
    role: '[Property manager — TBC]',
    rating: 5,
    avatar: { ratio: '1/1', label: 'Property manager who contracts TLB' },
  },
];

// §10 Comparison table — the brief said only "[Insert comparison table here,
// if applicable]", supplying no rows.
//
// REUSING the shared rows from src/data/comparison.ts, which is the opposite
// call to ndis-cleaning.astro (where the same table was ruled out as
// off-target for a participant). Here it fits better than on any other page:
// "employed by TLB, not subcontracted" is the exact payoff of the Award
// argument in "Worth knowing", "ongoing contracts with the region's leading
// real estate agencies" is on-target commercial proof, and "you see the
// cleaners who work your area" answers branded FAQ 2 directly. That copy was
// supplied directly by TLB and is already shared by two pages, so a third
// consumer costs nothing. Heading, cornerLabel and lead are framed for this
// page, as the component intends.
//
// ⚠️ Two shared row labels carry a trailing "*" with no footnote text
// supplied — a known open item on comparison.ts affecting all three
// consumers. The component's `footnote` prop is waiting for it; adding the
// text once fixes every page.
//
// ⚠️ OPEN DECISION, not taken here: adding a "Scope agreed in writing before
// we start" row. It is this page's own central promise and no existing row
// states it — but it isn't relevant to the homepage, so it would have to be
// a page-specific row rather than an addition to the shared set.
const comparisonTable = {
  heading: 'How TLB compares',
  cornerLabel: 'Why TLB Cleaning is different',
  lead: 'The things that decide whether a cleaning contract still works in month twelve.',
  columns: comparisonColumns,
  rows: comparisonRows,
};

// §11 Branded FAQs — all five ship, drafted in full, nothing bracketed.
// This is the cleanest FAQ block in any of the five briefs.
//
// ✅ All five are written in the CUSTOMER'S voice — "we" means the reader's
// business, not TLB, in four of the five questions. That is an unusual and
// effective register and it is consistent across the block. Preserve it in
// any additions; an answer later written in TLB's voice will read as bolted
// on.
//
// ⚠️ Two claims to confirm, neither blocking:
//   - "in TLB uniform" appears nowhere else on the site. If uniforms are
//     real this is a cheap, strong trust signal that belongs in more places
//     than one FAQ answer.
//   - "there's one local number to call" — footerContact.phone still renders
//     a literal [TBC] (navigation.ts:56). This page is the first to make an
//     explicit promise of a number the site doesn't yet display.
const brandedFaqItems = [
  {
    question: 'Are we too small a business for you?',
    answer:
      "Not at all — a two-person office matters as much to us as a large site. With a national chain, a small local job is a rounding error. Here, it's a neighbour's business.",
  },
  {
    question: "Who's actually in our building after hours?",
    answer:
      "The same small team you've met, in TLB uniform — never a roster of strangers sent by a call centre. You'll know their names, and there's one local number to call if anything's not right.",
  },
  {
    question: "We've used a big cleaning company before. What's different here?",
    answer:
      "You get the same people, every week, who know your site. Teagan runs TLB herself and her name's on the work — so there's someone who actually owns whether it's done properly.",
  },
  {
    question: 'What if the standard slips after a few months?',
    answer:
      'Tell us and we\'ll fix it — that\'s the deal. We agree the scope in writing so "done properly" means the same to both of us, and you can adjust the schedule anytime.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What happens if I get in touch?',
    answer:
      "We'll come and walk through your site, then send a written scope and price — no obligation. If it suits, we'll agree a start date and you'll meet the team who'll be cleaning.",
  },
];

// §12 Non-branded FAQs — all five drafted, none bracketed. Genuinely strong
// long-tail content, better than anything a local competitor will have.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFICATION PASS REQUIRED — lighter than the NDIS page's, but real.
// These claims are checkable and dated:
//   - Cleaning Services Award 2020 (MA000022) sets a legal minimum for
//     contract cleaners. Correct. Rates are reviewed ANNUALLY by the Fair
//     Work Commission — the claim is durable, any specific rate would not be.
//   - Early/late weekday shifts, weekends and public holidays attract
//     penalty rates. Correct in substance; confirm the phrasing covers shift
//     loadings vs. overtime accurately.
//   - Providers supply equipment, chemicals and safety data sheets. Correct
//     and standard — SDS provision is a WHS obligation, not a courtesy, and
//     could be stated more strongly.
//   - In NSW, child-related work requires a current Working With Children
//     Check and the engaging business must verify it. Correct under the
//     Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012 (NSW). ⚠️ SILENT ON
//     QLD's Blue Card — see the file header.
//   - "Most small offices settle on two to three cleans a week" is an
//     industry observation, not a rule, and is already hedged. Fine.
//
// Recommended before launch: a read by someone who prices commercial
// contracts (not a legal review — these are general statements, correctly
// hedged), and a visible "last reviewed" date on this block, because Award
// rates change every 1 July and undated content about pay goes stale
// silently.
//
// ⚠️ INTERNAL LINKS NOT ADDED. The plan wanted FAQ 2 to link /deep-cleaning/
// and FAQ 5 to link /schools-and-childcare-centres/, but Faq.astro renders
// `answer` as plain text ({item.answer} in a <p>), so inline links would
// need a change to a shared component's contract for one page's benefit.
// Not worth it: /deep-cleaning/ is already reachable from the header and
// footer, and /schools-and-childcare-centres/ is linked from the premises
// TagCloud above. Revisit if a second page needs rich FAQ answers.
const nonBrandedFaqItems = [
  {
    question: 'How often should an office be cleaned?',
    answer:
      'Most small offices settle on two to three cleans a week, moving to daily once headcount, foot traffic or client visits increase. Bathrooms, kitchens and high-touch surfaces need the most frequent attention regardless of the overall schedule. The right frequency depends on how hard the space is actually used — a quiet three-person studio and a busy café need very different plans. A site walkthrough settles it in ten minutes.',
  },
  {
    question: "What's the difference between a regular clean and a deep clean?",
    answer:
      "Regular cleaning covers the recurring tasks that keep a workplace usable: bins, bathrooms, kitchens, floors and high-touch surfaces on an agreed schedule. A deep clean is periodic and more intensive — carpet extraction, high dusting, detailed fittings, skirtings, vents and the edges routine visits don't reach. Most commercial sites run regular cleaning year-round and add a deep clean every few months, or after a fit-out or renovation.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Can commercial cleaning be done outside business hours?',
    answer:
      "Yes, and most of it is — before opening or after close, so your team isn't working around cleaners. After-hours work is usually priced differently, because weekday shifts that start early or finish late attract penalty rates under the Cleaning Services Award 2020, as do weekends and public holidays. Ask for both options on your quote so you can compare the real cost of each.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Who supplies the cleaning products and equipment?',
    answer:
      'The cleaner does. A commercial cleaning provider brings its own vacuums, mops, chemicals and safety data sheets as part of the service. Consumables are the thing to check separately — toilet paper, hand soap, paper towel and bin liners are sometimes included in the rate and sometimes billed on top. Get it written into the scope before you sign anything.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Do cleaners need a Working With Children Check?',
    answer:
      'Yes, where the work is child-related — a childcare centre, preschool, school or similar site. In NSW, child-related work requires a current Working With Children Check, and the business engaging the worker is responsible for verifying it. Ask any provider to confirm which of their team hold a current check before they attend a site where children are present.',
  },
];

// §13 Closing CTA — ⚠️ not in the brief, which has no closing-CTA row (and
// three mid-page rows reading "CTA -" with nothing after them).
//
// Lead and label come from branded FAQ 5, which describes what actually
// happens. "Book a site walkthrough" is a stronger offer for this buyer than
// an instant number would be, and unlike "Get an instant quote" it is true.
const closingCta = {
  kicker: 'Commercial Cleaning',
  heading: "Let's walk your site.",
  lead: "We'll come and walk through your site, then send a written scope and price — no obligation. If it suits, we'll agree a start date and you'll meet the team who'll be cleaning.",
  cta: { label: 'Book a site walkthrough', href: quoteCta.href },
};
---

<Base title={pageMeta.title} description={pageMeta.description} canonical={pageMeta.canonical} business={business}>
  <SiteHeader
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: logoImg }}
    primaryNav={primaryNav}
    serviceNav={headerNav}
    secondaryCta={headerSecondaryCta}
    cta={quoteCta}
  />

  <main id="main">
    <Hero
      variant="minimal"
      kicker={hero.kicker}
      headingLines={hero.headingLines}
      lead={hero.lead}
      cta={hero.cta}
    />

    <TrustBar items={trustBarItems} />

    <TextBlock theme="light" align="left" body={definitionBody} />

    <PathwayCards heading={pathwayCards.heading} headingAccent={pathwayCards.headingAccent} cards={pathwayCards.cards} />

    <TextBlock
      theme="dark"
      align="left"
      heading="Why two cleaning quotes for the same job can look nothing alike"
      body={worthKnowingBody}
    />

    <TagCloud
      heading="What we clean"
      subheading="Commercial cleaning across the Northern Rivers and the Tweed."
      tags={premisesTags}
      note={[]}
    />

    {/* ⚠️ THE SCOPE OF WORK BELONGS HERE AND DOES NOT EXIST.
        The block above tells the reader "the number that actually matters
        isn't the hourly rate — it's what's in the scope", and then asks them
        to demand a task list from any cleaner. Branded FAQ 4 promises "we
        agree the scope in writing". Non-branded FAQ 4 says "get it written
        into the scope before you sign anything". The page tells the reader
        to demand a scope four times and never shows one — which currently
        leaves TLB as the only cleaner on the page not showing its own.

        This is the highest-value missing content here, above even the
        definition paragraph, because it is what the page's own argument
        promises. It needs: what's done every visit, what's weekly vs.
        monthly, and whether consumables are in or out.

        NOT INVENTED. This is TLB's actual commercial scope and only they can
        state it. Build with ServiceBlocks (variant "list" or "icon-grid"),
        or a TextBlock with a { list } body entry if it's a plain task list.
        Card 2 above should anchor here once it exists. */}

    {/* §6 credibility signals, §7 hero video and §8 "the why" are all EMPTY
        in the brief — boilerplate labels with no direction at all. Not built
        and not guessed: deep-cleaning.astro's credibility block was filled
        on a reasonable assumption (before/after imagery) and removed again
        at TLB's direction, which is the precedent. Nothing can be borrowed
        from a sibling either — that copy is written for households choosing
        a cleaner, not for someone responsible for premises.

        "The why" is the notable absence. Its two obvious angles are already
        sitting unused in the FAQs: the embarrassment of a client walking
        into a dirty office, and the relief of not having to manage a
        cleaner. Both are stated better in the branded FAQs than anywhere
        else on the page. */}

    <ComparisonTable
      heading={comparisonTable.heading}
      cornerLabel={comparisonTable.cornerLabel}
      lead={comparisonTable.lead}
      columns={comparisonTable.columns}
      rows={comparisonTable.rows}
    />

    <TestimonialCarousel heading="What our clients say" testimonials={testimonials} uniform />

    <TagCloud
      heading="Where we clean"
      subheading="Across the Northern Rivers and the Tweed."
      tags={locationTags}
      note={[]}
    />

    <Faq heading="Frequently Asked Questions" items={brandedFaqItems} />
    <Faq heading="Commercial Cleaning FAQs" items={nonBrandedFaqItems} />

    <CallToAction
      variant="secondary"
      kicker={closingCta.kicker}
      heading={closingCta.heading}
      lead={closingCta.lead}
      cta={closingCta.cta}
    />
  </main>

  <SiteFooter
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: footerLogoImg }}
    columns={[
      { title: 'Services', links: footerServiceLinks },
      { title: 'Locations', links: [{ label: 'View all locations', href: '/locations/' }] },
    ]}
    contact={footerContact}
    copyright={footerCopyright}
  />
</Base>
